## A B S T R A C T
Keywords:   
2D Ni-MOFs/Cu-Zn0.5Cd0.5S   
Element doping   
Catalytic platform   
Synergistic effect   
Photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution
Doping strategy has been widely studied and applied to design visible light driven photocatalysts, which can introduce defect level and modulate the band structure of photocatalysts to improve the photocatalytic activity. In addition, it is of great significance to introduce a catalytic platform in the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution (PHE) system, which can also greatly improve the photogenerated carriers transport capacity. As is known to all, 2D materials have been proven to be crucial platform for PHE reactions. In view of this, we have successfully prepared the 2D Ni-MOFs/Cu-doped $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Cd}_{0.5}\mathrm{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{X}$ (abbreviated as NMCZ-x) heterojunction with the preferential orientation of crystals, which endows the catalyst with extremely excellent PHE activity and chemical stability. DFT calculations indicated that Cu atoms served as the center of electron capture after substituting $Z\mathbf{n}$ atoms, and Cu doping will introduce defect level. The NMCZ-10 complex achieves the best PHE activity and superior structural stability. This work highlights the importance of the synergistic effect between element doping and catalytic platform for the preparation of advanced photocatalysts.
## 1. Introduction
With the gradual exhaustion of fossil energy and more and more serious environmental problems, developing renewable and green en­ ergy is imperative. In recent years, hydrogen $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ energy, as a typical alternative energy source to fossil-fuel sources, has attracted worldwide attention owing to its higher energy density and smaller environmental pollution [1,2]. $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is considered as a promising energy due to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as its only combustion product with high calorific value of $122\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ [3–5]. As we all know, solar energy is inexhaustible and renewable. Therefore, among the many strategies for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production, photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evo­ lution (PHE) is considered as a promising technology to solve the current energy crisis [6–8]. At present, the biggest challenge for the PHE tech­ nology is to prepare green, efficient and stable photocatalysts.
Inspired by $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in 1972, not only metal oxides have been widely used in photocatalysis, but also metal sulfides have attracted much attention [9]. Among numerous metal sulfides, bimetallic sulfides $(\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Cd}_{0.5}S,$ , abbreviated as ZCS) emerges as a shining star due to the synergistic effects between bimetallic active centers, rich edge active sites and sufficient reduction potential [10]. Unpleasantly, pure ZCS is not an ideal photocatalyst due to its high carriers’ recombination rate, low photostability, low quantum efficiency, etc. [11,12]. To overcome the shortcomings of pure ZCS and improve its photocatalytic activity, various enhancement methods have been studied, such as construction of heterojunction [9,11], modification with noble metal-free [13,14], introduction of cocatalyst [15,16], element doping [10,17], and so on. Among these methods, element doping is regarded as a promising and simple method to improve the photocatalytic performance. For instance, She et al. reported a Ni-doped $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Cd}_{0.5}S$ photocatalyst. And the PHE tests showed that the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate over Ni-doped $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Cd}_{0.5}S$ was 6 times higher than pristine $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Cd}_{0.5}\mathrm{S}$ , indicating a significant enhancement in catalytic activity [18]. Tang et al. reported that nano­ porous Co-doped $\mathtt{Z n}_{\mathrm{1-x}}\mathrm{Cd}_{\mathrm{x}}S$ were facilely fabricated via adopting ZIFs as templates, and $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ and thiourea as precursors. The highly porous microstructure and uniform Co-doping of the photocatalyst afford a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -production rate (45.2 and 422.2 times larger than those of $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Cd}_{0.5}S$ and CdS) [19]. In addition, other dopants, such as $s_{\mathrm{m}}$ , Zn and Ag have also been proved to be active for the enhancement of PHE
