activity [20–22]. Generally speaking, the doping of foreign atoms can break the continuity of chemical field and then regulate the band structure of catalysts [23]. Apart from this, element doping could also introduce defect level via the d-orbitals hybridization of Cd, Zn and the metallic impurities [24]. And according to previous reports, Cu doping was believed to further strengthen the electronic and chemical features of semiconductor and the doped Cu could trap electrons and suppresses carrier’s recombination. Moreover, compared with other precious metals, Cu is an earth-abundant metal. Based on the above research background, we prepared the Cu-doped ZCS (abbreviated as CZ) pho­ tocatalyst, based on DFT calculations, the effect of Cu doping on the electronic structure of ZCS was discussed in detail, suggesting that the generation of defect level is beneficial to the improvement of PHE property.
Numerous evidences suggest that metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) materials and metal oxides have similar properties, such as being able to generate photogenerated carriers under photoexcitation. Metal ions are connected to oxygen ions in metal oxides, while organic links in MOFs and oxygen ions in metal oxides have the same role and function. Hence, we can think that the outer orbit of metal center constitutes the CB of MOFs, the outer orbit of the organic links composes the VB of MOFs [25,26]. In other words, MOFs possesses semiconductor-like behavior and has been widely researched and applied in removal of organic pollutants [27], PHE [28], photocatalytic $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction [29] and organic transformations [30]. Compared with inorganic semiconductor photocatalysts, MOFs materials are more advantageous due to multiple connections of metal center and organic linkers allow for fine tuning and design of photocatalysts at the molecular level. Moreover, intrinsic porosity of MOFs promotes the contact of catalysts and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ through the open framework during the PHE process. In addition, compared with 3D MOFs, 2D MOFs possesses more excellent properties, such as more exposed edge sites and faster carriers’ separation rate, which are more conducive to the improvement of photocatalytic activity. In recent years, 2D Ni-based MOFs has also attracted much attention [31,32].
As we all know, heterostructure photocatalysts have better catalytic performance than that of the parent phases because it can change the band bending at the interfaces and provide driving force for charge carriers’ migration and separation [33–35]. So, in order to augment the PHE activity of CZ, a series of strong coupled 2D Ni-MOFs/Cu-doped $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Cd}_{0.5}\mathrm{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{X}$ (abbreviated as NMCZ-x) composite photocatalysts with sandwich structure were prepared through interface engineering. Based on crystal structure, energy band position, photoelectrochemical prop­ erty and DFT calculation results, the possible photocatalytic mechanism was discussed in detail. The excellent PHE activity of NMCZ- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ can be attributed to the synergy effects between Cu doping and catalytic plat­ form. In addition, NMCZ- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ photocatalysts possess good reusability and ultrahigh structural stability. This work also shows that the 2D Ni-MOFs is a good photocatalytic platform to evidently raise the PHE activity of inorganic semiconductor photocatalysts.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Synthesis
## 2.1.1. Synthesis of $C Z-x$
CZ-x was synthesized by simple hydrothermal technology. The spe­ cific experimental steps are as follows: Zn $\mathrm{(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}{\cdot}2H_{2}O}$ $(0.274\ \mathrm{g})$ and Cd $\mathrm{(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H_{2}O}\left(0{.}333\mathrm{g}\right)$ were dissolved in $40\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized $\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ , then $10\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{Na_{2}S}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ (0.3 M) was added to the above solution, and kept stirring for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Next, a certain stoichiometric solution of Cu $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was added and stirred for $10~\mathrm{min}$ . Finally, the resulting suspension was sealed in a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ stainless steel autoclave, heated to $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and maintained for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . The obtained products were marked as CZ-0.05, CZ-0.1, CZ-0.15 and CZ-0.2 $\mathbf{\tilde{x}}\ =\ 0.05$ , 0.1, 0.15 and 0.2 represent the mass percentage of $\mathrm{Cu}\dot{}$ . The preparation of ZCS was the same as the above-mentioned hydrothermal method except that no Cu $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was added.
## 2.1.2. Synthesis of 2D Ni-MOFs
2D Ni-MOFs was prepared by hydrothermal, ultrasonic and me­ chanical grinding methods. Typically, Ni $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{(0.5~mmol)}$ , phenanthroline $\mathrm{(0.5~mmol)}$ , $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -Oxybis (benzoic acid) $\mathrm{(0.5~mmol)}$ , triethylamine $\mathrm{\cdot}1.0\ \mathrm{mmol})$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $\mathrm{10~mL}$ were dissolved in a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave, heated at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for six days. 3D Ni-MOFs were obtained after being washed with deionized $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [36]. Then, the 3D Ni-MOFs $(100~\mathrm{mg})$ were first mechanically ground for 30 min to powders. Subsequently, the obtained powders were added in absolute ethanol $\mathrm{100~mL}$ followed by ultrasonication for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . Finally, the dispersion was centrifuged at $3000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ to collect the 2D Ni-MOFs nanosheets.
## 2.1.3. Synthesis of NMCZ- $x$
The NMCZ- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ was fabricated by mechanically mixing the 2D Ni-MOFs ethanol solution $\left(2\mathrm{\mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\right)$ ) with the as-prepared CZ-0.1 in an agate mortar. First, CZ-0.1 $(100~\mathrm{mg})$ ) was added into the agate mortar, fol­ lowed by addition of a certain volume of 2D Ni-MOFs ethanol solution. Subsequently, the suspension was mechanically ground for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ to ensure that the ethanol solution was completely evaporated at room temperature. The volume of added 2D Ni-MOFs ethanol solution were $4.0~\mathrm{mL}$ , $5.0~\mathrm{mL}$ , $6.0~\mathrm{mL}$ and $7.0~\mathrm{mL}$ , respectively. Thus, the resulting samples were labelled as NMCZ-8, NMCZ-10, NMCZ-12 and NMCZ-14 $(\mathbf{x}=8$ , 10, 12 and 14 represent the mass percentage of 2D Ni-MOFs). The schematic diagram of the photocatalysts synthesis process is shown in Scheme 1.
