## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Phase composition analyses
XRD was carried out to study the crystal structure and purity of samples. As shown in Fig. 1a, the ZCS shows diffraction peaks at $27.09^{\circ}$ , $28.39^{\circ}$ , $45.01^{\circ}$ , $47.26^{\circ}$ , $53.24^{\circ}$ and $56.23^{\circ}$ , which can be indexed to (0 0 2), (1 0 1), (1 1 0), (1 0 3), (1 1 2) and (3 1 1) planes of the ZCS [13]. And the $Z_{\mathrm{{n/Cd/S}}}$ ratio in ZCS was determined by EDX, which demon­ strates the presence of Zn, Cd, and S and reveals the Zn: Cd: S molar ratio of 1:1:2 (Fig. S1 and Table S1). The results in Table S1 fully demonstrate that the phase composition of ZCS is $\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}\ \mathrm{Cd}_{0.5}\mathrm{S}$ . In different pro­ portions of $\mathbf{C}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{X},$ we observed the similar pattern as ZCS, which dem­ onstrates that the low content of Cu doping will not cause the formation of any impurity phases. Surprisingly, compared with the pure ZCS, the diffraction peaks of CZ- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ are slightly wider. According to the Scherrer formula, broader diffraction peaks mean smaller grain size [37]. This result shows that the Cu doping inhibits the grain growth of ZCS, which is beneficial to the improvement of PHE rate, because small size represents short migration distance and low recombination rate of charge carriers [37]. As shown in Fig. 1b, in the NMCZ- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotX}$ ternary com­ plex, we cannot observe the diffraction peak of 2D Ni-MOFs, indicating that the low content and high dispersion of 2D Ni-MOFs. In addition, the NMCZ- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotx}$ has a diffraction peak similar to that of CZ-0.1, indicating that the introduction of 2D Ni-MOFs does not change the crystal structure of CZ-x [36,38]. And what surprised us is that when the content of 2D NiMOFs gradually increases, the exposed crystal plane of ZCS changes from the original (0 0 2), (1 0 1), (1 1 0), (1 0 3), (1 1 2) and (3 1 1) to (1 0 0), (0 0 2), (1 0 1), (1 1 0) and (1 1 2). And as the content of 2D NiMOFs increases, the (10 0) crystal planes gradually appear, while the (101), (103) and (311) crystal planes gradually decrease or even disappear [13]. This phenomenon illustrates that the prepared samples have the preferred orientation of the (100) crystal planes. Based on the PHE activity and XRD results, we infer that the exposure of (1 0 1), (1 0 3) and (3 1 1) crystal planes is directly proportional to the PHE activity. In contrast, the exposure of the (1 0 0) crystal planes should be detrimental to the photocatalytic reaction. All in all, it is reasonable to think that the exposure of different crystal planes of semiconductor will greatly affect its photocatalytic activity. To further demonstrate the successful syn­ thesis of the NMCZ-x composite photocatalysts, EDX test was carried out. The EDX spectrum (Fig. 1c) of NMCZ-10 shows the presence of Zn, Cd, S, Cu, Ni, C, N and O. Based on XRD and EDX test results, we can make a preliminary judgment that the successful combination of 2D NiMOFs and CZ-0.1. And the presence of Cu and 2D Ni-MOFs in the NMCZ10 heterojunction photocatalysts has also been proved by the following TEM as well as XPS analysis.
## 3.2. Micromorphology analyses
The microstructure was characterized by TEM. As shown in Fig. 2a, the pure ZCS is composed of slightly aggregated nanoparticles (NPs). Fig. 2b shows the morphology of CZ-0.1, and we found that it is basically consistent with the morphology of ZCS, without major changes, indi­ cating that Cu doping did not affect the overall morphology of ZCS. Nanoparticle size distribution of ZCS and ${\tt C Z-0.1}$ are shown in Fig. S3. The size of ${\bf C}{\bf Z}{\bf-0.1}$ (the mean size of $6.26\mathrm{nm}$ ) is slightly smaller than that of ZCS (the mean size of $7.20~\mathrm{nm}\dot{}$ ) can be seen from Fig. S2, which is consistent with the phenomenon that Cu doping broadens the XRD diffraction peak of ZCS. Ni-MOFs possesses 2D nanosheet structure with smooth surface and small sheet thickness, as shown in Fig. 2c, d. The ultrathin 2D nanostructure often gives photocatalysts more active sites and higher carrier separation efficiency, thus improving the photo­ catalytic activity. Based on the TEM image in Fig. 2e, f, CZ-0.1 NPs are attached between the interlayers of 2D Ni-MOFs nanosheet, similar to a sandwich structure, rather than simply CZ-0.1 NPs are deposited on the surface of 2D Ni-MOFs nanosheet. In addition, the sandwich structure can guarantee the photocatalyst good structural stability. The HRTEM image of NMCZ-10 (Fig. 2g) shows the lattice spacing of $0.32\mathrm{nm}$ , which corresponds to the (002) crystal plane of ZCS [16]. The unchanged interplanar spacings may be due to the relative low Cu doping amount. At the same time, we can also observe the existence of 2D Ni-MOFs. And the intimate interaction was confirmed because no obvious boundary was observed between CZ-0.1 and 2D Ni-MOFs in HRTEM image, which can be beneficial to charge carriers’ migration and separation, conse­ quently, improve the PHE performance. Moreover, the sandwich struc­ ture remained intact even after ultrasonication treatment, which suggests that the heterostructure photocatalysts was prepared success­ fully rather than simple physical mixture between CZ-0.1 and 2D NiMOFs. Moreover, the HAADF-STEM images in Fig. 2h indicate that Zn, Cd, S, Cu, Ni, C, N and O were distributed throughout the NMCZ-10. The result is consistent with the EDX, indicating that the NMCZ-10 photo­ catalyst has been successfully prepared.
## 3.3. Surface chemical environment analyses
The XPS was performed to study the surface chemical states of photocatalysts. All the XPS peaks have been calibrated by the C 1s peak at 284.8 eV. The survey spectrum (Fig. 3a) shows the chemical composition of ZCS, CZ-0.1, 2D Ni-MOFs and NMCZ-10 samples. We found that due to the low Cu doping, the signal of Cu could not be obviously observed in the XPS survey spectrum of CZ-0.1. Similarly, in NMCZ-10, it is difficult to detect the presence of Ni due to the low content of 2D Ni-MOFs. In addition, the high-resolution spectra of $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ , Cd 3d, S 2p, Cu LM2 and Ni 2p are shown in Fig. 3b–f. In the Zn 2p XPS spectra (Fig. 3b), the binding energies of pure ZCS at $1021.82\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $1044.88\ \mathrm{eV}$ agree well with the values reported in the previous work, which correspond to Zn $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{zn\2p_{1/2}}$ of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , respectively [39,40]. For Cd 3d of pure ZCS, the peaks at $404.79\ \mathrm{eV}$ and 411.55 eV correspond to Cd $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Cd $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ of $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ , which are consistent with the reported [39,40]. And the S 2p of ZCS appears two characteristic peaks at $161.34~\mathrm{eV}$ and $162.49\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which can be attributed to S $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and S $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ [39,40]. From Fig. 3b–d, compared to ZCS, we found that the $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ , Cd 3d and $\mathtt{s2p}$ all shifted to high binding energy after Cu doping and the introduction of 2D Ni-MOFs in $\mathsf{C Z-}0.1$ and NMCZ-10, which shows that Cu doping can influence the chemical environments of $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ , Cd 3d and $\mathtt{s2p}$ in the ZCS and a decrease of surface electron density caused by the introduction of Cu atoms and 2D Ni-MOFs. Furthermore,
Cu LM2 (Fig. 3e) shows a characteristic kinetic energy at 918.01 eV, which directly indicated that the $+2$ -oxidation state of $\mathtt{C u}$ in CZ-0.1 and NMCZ-10 [41]. Fig. 3f displays the Ni 2p high-resolution spectra for 2D Ni-MOFs and NMCZ-10. In the 2D Ni-MOFs, the peaks at $855.59\mathrm{eV}$ and $873.04~\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to the Ni $\mathsf{2p3/2}$ and Ni $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ , as well as the cor­ responding satellite peaks at $860.37~\mathrm{eV}$ and $879.92\ \mathrm{eV}$ [36,42]. How­ ever, the Ni 2p signal for NMCZ-10 are too weak to be observed due to the low amount of Ni in NMCZ-10. The XPS results demonstrate that the CZ-0.1 and NMCZ-10 photocatalysts were successfully synthesized. More importantly, the changes of binding energies of different elements provide a theoretical basis for the analysis of photocatalytic mechanism.
## 3.4. PHE activity and stability
The PHE activity of different samples are shown in Fig. 4a, b. Bare ZCS reveals the poor PHE activity due to the fast recombination of charge carriers, and the PHE rate is about $922.6~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}\cdot\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ . The good news is that the CZ-x photocatalysts significantly improve the PHE activity. As the Cu content increases, the PHE activity first increases and then decreases, which can be attributed to that the light screening effect caused by excessive Cu doping. On the other hand, the excessive amount of Cu could also become the recombination center of photogenerated carriers, thereby decreasing the separation rate of carriers and leading to the reduction of PHE activity [43,44]. Among them, CZ-0.1 possesses the best photocatalytic performance and the PHE rate can reach 2155.4 $\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{\Pi}}\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A}}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{o l}\cdot\mathsf{g}^{-1}\cdot\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ . Then, in order to further improve the PHE activity, NMCZ- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotX}$ were successfully prepared by combining 2D Ni-MOFs and CZ0.1. In the same way, as the 2D Ni-MOFs content increases, the PHE activity first increases and then decreases, which indicated that the mass ratio of the components is related to the PHE performance. NMCZ-10 is the best photocatalyst, and its PHE rate is as high as 5771.2 $\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{\Pi}}\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A}}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{o l}\cdot\mathsf{g}^{-1}\bar{\mathsf{\Pi}}\bar{\mathsf{\Pi}}^{-1}$ , which is 6.26 and 2.68 times that of pure ZCS and CZ0.1. Although pure 2D Ni-MOFs is not active for PHE, it plays a pro­ moting role in the PHE process, so we have reason to believe that 2D NiMOFs is a co-catalyst with good development prospects. As displayed in Fig. $4\upepsilon$ , the PHE activity was researched under different wavelengths of light $420~\mathrm{nm}$ , $450~\mathrm{nm}$ , $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $550~\mathrm{nm}^{\cdot}$ ). And apparent quantum efficiency (AQE) is $14.3\%$ at $420\ \mathrm{nm}$ monochromatic light, which matches well with the DRS spectrum (Fig. 8a), suggesting that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was generated by the photocatalytic reaction. As the wavelength increases, the AQE of NMCZ-10 significantly decreases, which indicates that the PHE rate of NMCZ-10 has a desirable dependence on the wavelength of incident light [45,46]. Correspondingly, the TPR of NMCZ-10 (Fig. 4d)
at different wavelengths agrees well with the result of AQE [45,46]. As shown in Fig. 4d, the photocurrent density is the highest at $420~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , followed by $450\mathrm{nm}$ , $500\mathrm{nm}$ and $550\mathrm{nm}$ . In different proportions of the complex, since CZ-0.1 and NMCZ-10 have relatively high PHE activity, they are selected as representative substances for the following char­ acterization analysis.
In regard to the common serious issue, photocorrosion, for sulfides, the stability of NMCZ-10 was investigated. Fig. 5a–f shows the chemical stability and reusability of NMCZ-10 sample. Six PHE cycles (five hours per cycle, Fig. 5a) were carried out under the same experimental con­ ditions. The PHE rate slightly decreased after three cycles due to the TEOA was continuously consumed by holes. Fortunately, the PHE rate was restored when an additional $4~\mathrm{mL}$ of TEOA was added to the pho­ tocatalytic reaction system, suggesting that the NMCZ-10 sample pos­ sesses good stability and reusability. Importantly, the cycle performance is not comprehensive and reliable evidence to measure whether photo­ corrosion has occurred, because of other factors such as active site blocking, loss of sample and formation of intermediates could also lead to a decrease in cycle performance. Therefore, in order to determine the stability of photocatalysts more accurately, the crystal structure and surface properties of the reused samples were tested by XRD and XPS. As shown in Fig. 5b, the XRD result shows that the crystal structure of NMCZ-10 has no apparent change before and after the six PHE cycles. In addition, from XPS spectra (Fig. 5c–f) of fresh and used NMCZ-10 pho­ tocatalyst, they display the high similarity, just the difference in the intensity and peak width. Most important of all, the S 2p XPS spectrum show that no $s^{0}$ was detected, which further explained the stability of the photocatalyst. All in all, in addition to six PHE cycles, XRD and XPS tests further illustrate the good chemical stability and recyclability of the photocatalyst. The excellent stability of photocatalysts can be mainly attributed to the synergistic effect between Cu doping and 2D Ni-MOFs as a catalytic platform, which can prevent the carriers’ recombination and retard the oxidation reaction between holes and $\displaystyle{s^{2-}}$ in the ZCS. In addition, the sandwich structure of the photocatalysts gives it structural stability.
## 3.5. Photoelectrochemical analyses
In order to study the charge transfer dynamics and enhanced surface reaction of different photocatalysts, we conducted a series of photo­ electrochemical tests. First, PL and TRPL decay were conducted. From Fig. 6a, the intensity order of the emission peaks is $\mathrm{ZCS}>\mathrm{CZ}{\cdot}0.1>$ NMCZ-10. This result shows that the pure ZCS has a high photogenerated carriers recombination probability. With the Cu doping and the introduction of 2D Ni-MOFs, the PL intensity becomes lower and lower, indicating that the separation efficiency of charge carriers is greatly improved, which is helpful to improve the PHE activity [47–49]. Furthermore, as depicted in Fig. 6b–d, the emission lifetimes of ZCS, CZ0.1 and NMCZ-10 are 1.02 ns, 1.37 ns and 2.57 ns. The increased life­ time of CZ-0.1 and NMCZ-10 were attributed to the efficient separation and transfer of photogenerated carriers, which caused by the generation of defect level and the introduction of catalytic platform [50,51]. The long lifetime allows electrons to have a longer time to migrate to the catalyst surface, thus increasing the number of electrons involving in the PHE reaction, which is conducive to the improvement of PHE activity. Since electrochemical tests can directly reflect the separation and recombination of photogenerated charge carriers, we also conducted TPR [52], EIS [53] and SPV [16] tests. Fig. 6e, f display the TPR and EIS results of different samples. It can be found that the NMCZ-10 shows enhanced photocurrent intensity and smaller semicircle than ZCS and CZ-0.1, suggesting that NMCZ-10 nanocomposites has a faster photogenerated carriers’ separation efficiency. In addition, SPV (Fig. 6g) was employed to reveal the dynamic behaviors of the photogenerated charge carriers in photocatalysts. Generally speaking, the stronger photovoltage signal is representative of higher photogenerated charges separation efficiency. As expected, SPV test result is consistent with the results of PL, TRPL, TPR and EIS. The efficient restriction of carriers’ recombination caused by the synergy between Cu doping and catalytic platform in NMCZ-10, are very beneficial for PHE. In addition, the overpotential of PHE is also closely related to the reaction activity. The LSV technology was applied to analyze the PHE overpotentials of different samples in Fig. 6h. LSV test result exhibits that the over­ potential of NMCZ-10 is the lowest among these samples, suggesting that the NMCZ-10 is capable of rapid hydrogen evolution under very small overpotential conditions. As we all know, the photocatalytic reaction mainly includes three processes: (1) the generation of photogenerated electron-hole pairs under the excitation of the light source; (2) the migration of photogenerated electron-hole pairs to the catalyst surface; (3) the photogenerated electron-hole pairs generates an efficient redox reaction on the surface of catalyst. Through the above mentioned pho­ toelectrochemical tests, the increase in PHE activity can be attributed to the accelerated carrier migration rate and enhanced surface chemical reaction. The composite photocatalyst not only accelerates the separa­ tion and migration rate of photogenerated carriers, but the ultra-low overpotential is also more conducive to the occurrence of redox re­ actions. In short, NMCZ-10 composite may demonstrate exceptional efficiency in PHE.
## 3.6. Physisorption property analyses
BET specific surface area $(S_{\mathrm{BET}})$ and BJH pore-size distributions have an important influence on the photocatalytic activity [54]. As we all know, large $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ can promote surface reactions by offering more cata­ lytic active sites and accelerating mass transport. As shown in Fig. 7a, the isotherms of these samples are typical type-IV and the hysteresis loops are detected between 0.7 and 1.0 $\left(P/P_{0}\right)$ . And the SBET of ZCS, CZ0.1 and NMCZ-10 are $75.193~\mathrm{~m}^{2}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $89.082~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and 100.58 $\mathbf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . In comparison to pure ZCS, the $\boldsymbol{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ of CZ-0.1 and NMCZ-10 are significantly improved. Fig. 7b shows the corresponding pore size dis­ tribution curves of ZCS, CZ-0.1 and NMCZ-10, indicating that the fea­ tures of mesoporous structure. In addition, Fig. 7b also shows that ZCS and CZ-0.1 have similar BJH pore diameter distributions, suggesting that the original structure of ZCS is preserved after Cu doping. According to the above results, we have reason to speculate that the NMCZ-10 is more beneficial to the improvement of PHE rate. Based on the result of PHE activity, we can think that the $\ensuremath{S_{\mathrm{BET}}}$ is one of the factors influencing the improvement of PHE activity in this system.
## 3.7. Photocatalytic mechanism analyses
The optical absorption properties of ZCS, CZ-0.1, 2D Ni-MOFs and NMCZ-10 were analyzed through UV–vis DRS (Fig. 8a). The 2D Ni-MOFs only responds to UV light before ${386}\mathrm{nm}$ . However, the absorption edge of pure ZCS locates at about $530~\mathrm{nm}$ , indicating the good visible light response ability. Compared with ZCS, the absorption edge of ${\bf C}{\bf Z}{\bf-0.1}$ is slightly redshift, indicating that the Cu doping can change the band gap structure due to the formation of defect level. And the CZ-0.1 sample has a slight absorption tail due to the Cu doping. Compared with ZCS, the CZ-0.1 sample may have an enhanced PHE activity. When CZ-0.1 and 2D Ni-MOFs combine to form NMCZ-10, the response of NMCZ-10 to visible light slightly decreases. The result of UV–vis DRS shows that the response to visible light is not the main factor in improving PHE activity. As shown in Fig. 8b, the $\mathbf{E_{g}}$ of ZCS, CZ-0.1, 2D Ni-MOFs and NMCZ-10 are 2.34 eV, 2.31 eV, 3.21 eV and $2.45\ \mathrm{eV}_{:}$ , respectively. Fig. 8c dis­ plays the VB-XPS spectra near the Fermi level [55,56]. The flat-band potential $\left(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{FB}}\right)$ of the ZCS, CZ-0.1 and 2D Ni-MOFs were measured by Mott-Schottky plots at different frequencies. As shown in Fig. 8d–f, the ZCS, CZ-0.1 and 2D Ni-MOFs samples all possess a positive slope, sug­ gesting that they are all n-type semiconductor and the Cu doping does not change the intrinsic semiconductor property of ZCS. The $\mathbf{E}_{\mathrm{FB}}$ (vs. Ag/ AgCl) of ZCS ( 1.11 V), CZ-0.1 ( 1.16 V) and 2D Ni-MOFs $(-0.8\mathrm{V})$ can be converted into $-0.91\mathrm{~V~}$ , $-0.96{\mathrm{~V}}$ and $-0.6\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs. NHE). For n-type semiconductor, it is generally considered that the $\mathbf{E}_{\mathrm{FB}}$ is $0.1~\mathrm{V}$ higher than $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{CB}}$ . Hence, the $\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{CB}}$ of ZCS, CZ-0.1 and 2D Ni-MOFs were estimated to be $-1.01\mathrm{~V~}$ , $-1.06\mathrm{~V~}$ and $-0.7\mathrm{~V~}$ , respectively. And the EVB of ZCS (1.33 V), CZ-0.1 (1.25 V) and 2D Ni-MOFs (2.51 V) were obtained by formula: $\mathrm{E_{g}=E_{V B}-E_{C B}}$ .
In order to illustrate the effect of Cu doping more accurately, the DFT calculations were carried out by building ideal models (Fig. 9a and e). The density of states (DOS) of ZCS and Cu-doped ZCS are shown in Fig. 9b and f. Compared with pure ZCS, it can be clearly seen that the DOS image of Cu-doped ZCS appears a defect level below the CB, the existence of defect level can shorten the transfer distance of charge carriers and suppress the recombination of photogenerated carriers in the PHE reaction. Furthermore, Fig. 9c shows that pure ZCS is a direct band gap photocatalyst with $\mathrm{E_{g}}=2.361$ eV at point G. However, the $\mathbf{E_{g}}$ of Cu-doped ZCS is $2.302\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. $9\mathrm{g}$ . The decrease of $\mathbf{E_{g}}$ can be attributed to that the doped Cu causes the movement of CB and VB and the generation of defect level, which can effectively improve the PHE activity. Accordingly, the position changes of CB and VB before and after Cu doping are consistent with the experimental results. The calculated CB and VB of pure ZCS is $-1.017\mathrm{eV}$ and $1.344\mathrm{eV}$ . And the calculated CB and VB of Cu-doped ZCS is $-1.073\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $1.229~\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. In addition, differential charge density was calculated to analyze the Cu doping-induced electron density redistribution. DFT results (Fig. 9d and h) exhibit that the surrounding electron density can be enhanced when Cu substituted $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms, which indicates that the doped Cu could trap electrons. The electron density redistribution will lead to the formation of internal electric field and then benefitted the separation of charge carriers. The calculated result of differential charge density is consistent with the above XPS result. In conclusion, the DFT calculations are in
C. Li et al.
agreement with a series of experimental results, indicating the reliability of the calculated method and model.
Although the $\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{CB}}$ $(-1.01\mathrm{V})$ of ZCS is much more negative than the $\mathrm{EH}^{+}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , the PHE performance of pristine ZCS is still unsatisfactory due to rapid recombination of charge carriers, as well as high overpotential for PHE. In this work, the $\operatorname{EcB}$ of CZ-0.1 is more negative than ZCS, indicating that the CZ-0.1 may has a higher photoreduction capability. Correspondingly, the oxidative ability of holes becomes lower due to the upward movement of the VB positions, which inhibits the oxidation of $s^{2-}$ . Based on the above experimental results and DFT calculations, the possible PHE mechanism is shown in Scheme 2. As for ZCS, electrons can transfer from the VB to the CB under photoexcitation, leaving holes in the VB. Then $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ can be reduced by the electrons in the CB and more electrons will recombine with holes quickly, which is also the main reason for the low catalytic activity of the pure ZCS. However, after the CZ-0.1 was excited by visible light, the generated defect level $\mathrm{(E_{D})}$ as the driving force for charge separation that prevents the recombination of photogenerated electrons-holes pairs and drives the targeted surface reaction in the PHE systems. Hence, compared with ZCS, CZ-0.1 has a higher PHE ability. Before the contact between CZ-0.1 and 2D Ni-MOFs, the $\mathbf{E}_{\mathrm{FB}}$ of ${\bf C}{\bf Z}{\bf-0.1}$ is higher than 2D Ni-MOFs. When excited by visible light, after CZ-0.1 and 2D Ni-MOFs contacted to form a type II hetero­ junction, the EFB of CZ-0.1 and 2D Ni-MOFs moved down and up until balanced. And the electrons were transferred from the CB of CZ-0.1 to the 2D Ni-MOFs. And subsequently these electrons further migrated to Pt, then it reduced the $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ to generate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Meanwhile, the photo­ generated holes aggregated on the VB for CZ-0.1 were sacrificed by TEOA. As a result, significantly enhanced PHE activity and good chemical stability can be achieved.
## 4. Conclusion
This work reports for the first time that 2D Ni-MOFs served as cat­ alytic platform to change the exposed lattice plane of CZ-0.1 and greatly raise the PHE activity of main catalyst CZ-0.1. By tuning the content of 2D Ni-MOFs, NMCZ- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ developed highly preferred orientation along (1 0 0). Astonishingly, the NMCZ-10 exhibits excellent PHE activity of $5771.2\upmu\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}\cdot\mathbf{h}^{-1}$ under the condition of TEOA as electron donor, which is 6.26 and 2.68 times that of ZCS and CZ-0.1. The enhanced PHE activity can be ascribed to the synergy between Cu doping and catalytic platform in NMCZ- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ , which endows the composite photocatalyst with higher photogenerated carriers’ separation efficiency, lower over­ potential of PHE and larger specific surface area. Apart from this, what’s more exciting is that the NMCZ- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotX}$ photocatalyst with sandwich structure has good chemical stability in successive PHE process. The development of this work provides new ideas and research directions for solving the photocorrosion behavior of sulfides. Furthermore, compared with inorganic semiconductor, MOFs exhibits more possibilities in designing novel materials for PHE due to the diverse choice of metal nodes and organic linkers as well as flexible coordination mode. This work also provides new insights into the development of MOFs materials in the field of PHE, and elaborate on the mechanism of the combination of MOFs and inorganic semiconductors for PHE.
